Yes. I compared the **September 4, 2020 snapshot** with the **December 28, 2019 snapshot**, focusing especially on the provisions relevant to the March outage.

### Bottom line

The September snapshot does **not** show BitMEX removing the provisions that are most useful to your argument. In September—almost six months after the March event and more than three months after the May outage—BitMEX still said:

* it was BitMEX's **“responsibility to maintain an orderly market”**;
* BitMEX could **halt trading** because of market disruption or external events;
* BitMEX would make **“reasonable efforts”** to ensure services were available;
* BitMEX would use **“commercially reasonable efforts”** to avoid downtime during anticipated peak hours;
* its affiliated trading arm supposedly received access and trading privileges on the **same terms as other users**. ([Wayback Machine][1])

That's useful.

There **were changes elsewhere**, however, and one in particular deserves more investigation.

